<div class="tab-guide-body" data-guide-body="guide_body_track_record" hidden data-i18n-html="guide_body_track_record">
  <p>Both numbers are <strong>excess return over ACWI</strong> for the horizon
  selected above — the strategy's return minus the benchmark's over the same
  window. The unit is <strong>percentage points (pp)</strong>, not percent,
  because it is the gap between two percentages: if the strategy returned
  3.1% and ACWI returned 1.9%, the strategy is <strong>1.2 percentage
  points</strong> ahead.</p>
  <p>They are <strong>point-in-time</strong>: the backtest curve reflects the
  themes the strategy actually held at each historical rebalance, including
  the hysteresis band. It is not today's picks measured backwards, which
  would credit the strategy with picks it had not made.</p>
  <h3>Why two windows</h3>
  <p>One month of a five-holding thematic strategy is mostly noise. The same
  strategy on the same day can read strongly positive over one month and
  strongly negative over twelve, or the reverse — the window picks the
  story. Both are shown so neither is read alone.</p>
  <p>Returns are net of the round-trip trading cost assumption, the same
  caveat the Backtest tab carries.</p>
</div>
